Due crossposting of this with different Subject to other Newsgroup I didn't
notice this topic here:

>Where can one find out more about the DEC Advanced File System? I
>assume standard tools (dump/restore) can be used to backup such file
>systems?

AdvFS (Advanced File System) comes with DEC OSF/1 V1.3 distribution.

Some features of AdvFS are:

For free with DEC OSF/1:
	- log based file system
	- no need for fsck
	- fast recovery from errors (powerfail, crashes etc.),
	  typically less than 10s in all cases
	- dump/restore interface for back up needs (extended dump/restore)
	

With Advanced File System Utilities License:
	- system management command set
	- large volume sets (can be set up as single or multiple "partitions")
	- multiple partitions over volume set
	- migrating files over selected volumes
	- monitoring and control commands for file migration and fragmentation
	- dynamic partition/file set/volume set growing by adding new volumes
	- adding/removing devices from set "on the fly"
	- file level striping
	- on line backups via cloning
	- undelete of deleted files

	There's some new terminology compared to std. UFS, but in general
	AdvFS frees you from partition size and growth limitations of UFS.
	You build a file domain from one or more disk volumes, and then 
	you create one or more filesets under that domain. Later you can
	dynamically add/remove physical disks to/from that domain
	and also you can restrict fileset diskusage with quotas etc.
	Filesets act as "replacement" of partition. Personally I've found
	AdvFS easy to use and VERY useful in environments needing dynamic
	partitioning and using large number of volumes. Also I've personally
	strongly recommended usage of AdvFS in environments where customer
	is using High Availability solutions like DECwatchdog/Autopilot. Only
	drawback I can recall right now, is that AdvFS doesn't support root
	partition YET. IMHO: AdvFS also helps in system management.

Software Product Description (SPD) gives more details. SPD number is #44.52.00
and can be found either from gatekeeper.dec.com:pub/DEC/DECinfo/SPD/ or by 
asking your local Sales Rep to provide it. Also SPD of DEC OSF/1 V1.3 might
be interesting (#41.61.03). Gatekeeper doesn't necessarily have all the latest
SPD's on line yet...

