A couple of weeks ago, I posted an article asking about software
distribution devices for DEC OSF/1 for AXP, particularly tape devices.

Before I get to the summary, a bit of background is in order...

First off, my interest in tape devices was not because I like tape
devices or anything strange like that. Rather, it was based on the
assumption (which may not be as valid as I thought) that CD-ROM was
impractical for low-volume and/or custom applications. In retrospect,
I should have been a little more clear about this. When I said custom,
I *really* meant that. We will be distributing volumes as low as 1. (I
guess "volumes" in that context is somewhat of an oxymoron.)

As for *which* tape devices, my preconception, which was proven to be
wrong, was that the QIC would be preferred, based on the following
reasoning:

 - DAT isn't a universal backup medium (e.g., 8mm is also prevalent),
   it's not inexpensive, and it's usually locked up inside a computer
   room, so it wouldn't be the best device for software distribution.

 - Everyone seems to hate the TK50, and not every AXP site would
   have one, so it wouldn't be acceptable for software distribution.

 - There are a lot of QICs around, and the QIC is somewhat of a
   standard (at least for other vendors' UNIXes), so it would be
   preferred. 

The responses I received were quite interesting and make reasonable
sense.

 - No one who responded thought QIC would be acceptable.

 - Not very many consumers are interested even remotely in software
   distribution on tape. Not surprisingly, virtually everyone wants
   CD-ROM. (Digital ensures that everyone has a drive by only
   distributing on CD-ROM.)

 - Devices capable of mastering CD-ROM seem to be becoming more
   practical (read, less expensive), even for low volumes.

 - Since everyone does backups and Digital provides a built-in DAT
   device, DAT is the tape medium most likely to be available at AXP
   sites.

 - A smattering of people would like TK50 or 8mm.

Live and learn... I sure like the Net.

Thanks very much to all who responded.

My original article, followed by the email responses I received are
appended below.

