For planning purposes, I would like to know what sort of tape device
is preferred for software distribution for applications for DEC OSF/1
for AXP, in particular, for workstations.

I am told by Digital that CD is the preferred software distribution
device, in general. Personally, I would think that CD is neither
practical nor cost-effective for low-volume and/or custom software.

I am also told that the TLZ06 (i.e., 4mm DAT) is used as a software
distribution device by some ISVs since it is standard on some
workstation configurations. Personally, I would think DAT would be
overkill for software distribution, since it is really a backup
device.

Remaining alternatives are the TZ30 (aka, TK50) and the TZK10 (aka,
QIC). Given that the rest of the industry seems to have adopted the
QIC, one would think it would be preferrable to the TZ30.

If you either sell or purchase applications for DEC OSF/1 for AXP,
what is/was the media? If you plan to either sell or purchase, what
would you prefer? Responses from Digital are welcome.

Please reply by email, as I will be out of the office for the next
week or so. If you are an ISV, please indicate whether you would
object to being included in a summary.

All responses will be appreciated.  I will summarize results and post
either in a couple of weeks or as soon as a trend is obvious.

Thanks in advance.

