I work for a systems integrator. It seems that 4mm DAT is definitely the
way to go, not really 'overkill' at all:

a. Cost - For $15 a cartridge, who cares if some space is unused?
   TK50 and QIC cost more, 8mm is on par.
b. Capacity - You can pack a CDROM or most SCSI drives [2+ GB] onto
   a single tape, very handy in a support environment.
c. Support - DEC supports this format directly. The 8mm and QIC
   solutions are resold, but not a key technology for DEC.
   This is particularly important with issues such as getting the drive
   to work on an Infoserver [per other threads in this newsgroup].

The key is to get DEC to support DAT as a bootable device. Not everyone
has CDROM, and in cost-sensitive areas it's nice to have the load and
backup device be the same.

So there's my straw poll two cents! Thanks for asking.

