Which again means it's likely the customer has [access to] one.  Most 
machines around here have either a 4mm or 8mm drive for backup purposes.  
Those that do not, have remote access to those that do.

>Remaining alternatives are the TZ30 (aka, TK50) and the TZK10 (aka,
>QIC). Given that the rest of the industry seems to have adopted the
>QIC, one would think it would be preferrable to the TZ30.

We have approx half a dozen DECstations in our workgroup; none were 
purchased with a TK50 drive.  These drives are _old_ technology; IMHO 
their capacity makes them useless for anything but software distribution.
Should a site be forced to own one for that reason alone ?  

Ditto for QIC's; we've not purchased a machine so equipped in about four 
years.

In my experience, most smaller/newer site admins feel this way.  We 
_have_ cdrom drives for OS and compiler installations.  We _have_ large 
capacity tape drives for doing our backups.  A TK50 or QIC tape might be 
a less expensive distribution media (I doubt it), but I'd have to buy a lot 
of TK50's vs DAT's or CD's before the savings would cover the purchase cost 
of an otherwise unused drive.

Of course, I am taking largely of smaller or newer sites; larger or longer 
established ones will undoubtably have TK50 and QIC drives on hand.  Given
that you're talking about a new platform like the Alpha, however, I cannot
see using one of these older media.  I am quite sure the number of Alphas
equipped with cdrom/4mm/8mm will grossly outnumber those which additionally
feature TK50's or QIC's.

Just my strongly opinionated $0.02 worth (I hate TK50's! :-)  Please do
summerize your results to the Net when you're through.

Cheers,

