Frequency:

a) Since Digital (and most other vendors) now are using CDs as their
   primary distribution format, most sites will have a CD-ROM player.
   Say 80-90% of all sites.
b) Most sites makes backups. I would guess (for the Alpha) that 50-60%
   have at least one 4mm DAT station, and 40-50% have at least one
   8mm video (exabyte) station. (Seems that Digital is actively
   pushing the DAT, but for backups I like the 8mm better.)
c) Many have an TK50 stashed away somewhere, most have some QIC tape
   devices - 60MB and 150MB is almost a sure bet, larger is probably
   rare. I'd guess at 30-40% for 150MB QIC tape device.

Cost:
a) The cost of making a CD-ROM is said to have come down a lot, but
   I don't know what that means - maybe it's just 'almost affordable',
   maybe it's quite cheap. Hope somebody else can tell you this.
b) backup tapes - 8mm and 4mm - are quite cheap, I just bought some
   DATs at ~($ 15/tape), and that's in the nowhere of Trondheim, Norway,
   in small quantity. 8mm tapes are even cheaper (of course I'm
   talking about the computer backup tape type, not video quality
   tapes), last I checked.
c) QIC tapes have been more expensive here, I'd say around $30/tape
   at least. You obviously need to check what the cost is at
   your end of the world (now and in 6 months - note prices may
   change fast!)

Depending on number of copies you expect to distribute and the
costs, I'd say go for the 4mm or the CD track. Consider making
patches available via the Net (or modem connection), if you
get a frozen version on CD that you aren't quite happy with.

Note: I'm just a SA with some experiences running around with
quirky tapes trying to find somewhere to read them, so I'm
only seeing what we have available here, and guessing about the
rest of the world.

Good luck,

