  Is there a memory leak somewhere? If so, are they patches?

If you look in the release notes that came with the CD/ROM there's a
work around for the memory leak.  The way the X11R4 and later servers
from MIT come is that when all the clients die, they try to reset themselves
and shrink back down to their initial memory.  There is a bug in the DEC
server that eats 2M with ever login that doesn't get reclaimed.  The simple
workaround is to fix the XDM startup to restart the server each time.
They show you how to do it in the release notes.


Another place to look for things consuming swap space is shared memory regions.
These preallocate a large hunk of swap for their operation.  Do "ipcs."  I've
noticed a bug that for some reason, if you abort a program being traced (i.e.,
you were running dbx), the shared memory region doesn't go away even if you
had already deleted it's identifier and it should have gone away because the
attach reference count had gone to zero.

The C compiler seems to be a memory pig as well, can't comment on EMACS.

-Ron


