I consider strongly-typed languages an advance; Ada would be my favorite
here.  What I like about Ada is that it allows me to express the semantics
of my program in terms the compiler can understand and check - I find that
I can get large Ada applications running correctly sooner than with other
languages, and I find very few bugs later.  C++ has many of the advantages
of Ada, but it also has all of the disadvantages of C.  Still, C++ is
another significant advance in 3GLs, if used properly (and not as C with
additional obscure syntax.)  

As for talking to computers, I think this is a lot harder than people
thought even 10 years ago.  Last week's Doonesbury comic strip, which
skewered the Apple Newton, accurately illustrates this.  Egg freckles?
(I watched as someone here, who had just bought a Newton, spent about half
an hour trying to show someone how to enter her birthday in the appointment
book - she hadn't managed to do it yet as I left.)

Where we have made big advances is in graphical interfaces.  I was blown away
by what I saw when I visited an oil-industry customer who was using AVS to put 
together a production tool to help their geologists visualize where oil
pockets might be located given the topography of the area.  By combining
predefined input and output "widgets" and filters, along with some
Fortran code, they could rapidly deliver a complex tool with an easy to 
understand (and customizable!) interface.   This is where I see programming
heading, but I suspect that "traditional" languages will still be important
even 10 and 20 years from now - where the advances in programming styles are
coming is in making computers more accessible to more people.
