In article <CCC5CK.B5D@irvine.com>, jsweet@irvine.com (Jerry Sweet) writes:
> For those who don't know to what I'm referring, there are companies
> that specialize in getting as much data as possible off disks that
> have otherwise gone to the place where disks are eternally blessed.
> 
> They probably charge so much money for their services that it's not
> worth it for us, but I'd still like to find out who they are and 
> what they charge.

  It's pretty much a "whatever the customer will pay" market - and the
answer is usually "a lot". Prices go down as the environment becomes more
popular. However, VMS is not a major market for this stuff.

  I've been subcontracted for some recovery work in the past. I've been
paid between $1,000 and $2,000 per case. This is for a single 256Mb disk
drive. I assume that the recovery house I did this for got at least twice
that. Before you think it's a great line of work to get into, I had to
build my own hardware for recovery (ever see a disk drive with a crank
instead of a voice coil?) and it's on a "drop everything" basis - I nor-
mally have to be done within a few hours so the pack and tapes can get
back on an airplane.

