In article <257e8mINNoh0@gap.caltech.edu>, 
ktl@wag.caltech.edu (Kian-Tat Lim) writes...
>	I once heard a rumor that this was in fact a *design feature*,
>intended to ensure that all of Digital's language products remained
>supported and interoperable.  Strange but true, or strange but false?

Strange but false.  Though it happens that many languages (I've found Fortran,
Pascal, BASIC, PL/I and Ada, and of course Cm but no COBOL or RPG) are used in
various VMS components, this was due more to the component developer's
familiarity with the language and that language's suitability to the task. 
That VMS doesn't have a pronounced "bias" towards any one language has been a
major benefit since its inception.

However, we don't need to have VMS written in "one of each" language in order
to preserve the common language environment - we do extensive testing,
as well as have designs for new features reviewed by developers for all of
the languages, to keep things from sliding.

Back around 1985, VMS management did look into whether or not VMS should
standardize on one language, and/or select a small set of languages which
could be used, but it was determined that at the time no one language
stood out as being superior to others for the purpose.  BLISS was the
non-assembler language of choice, but other languages could be (and
have been) used as appropriate.  Curiously, Ada was called out as having
the most promise as a systems programming language, but it was just
"too new" at the time.

My personal belief is that the strong industry shift towards C is a
mistake, as it is simply an "assembler for the 1970s", but it's better
than assembler itself.  There are many at Digital who feel that if BLISS
had been included with VMS that it would have risen to the prominence that
C has today (not that BLISS is perfect either - it and C are very similar -
but at least BLISS programmers don't try to pretend that they're writing
in a high-level language.)

For better or for worse, more and more Digital software will be written
in C (and C++, which, if used properly, can have significant benefits.)
I do welcome the gradual eradication of assembler wherever possible.

