In article <250jvh$j14@sol.TIS.COM>, mjr@tis.com (Marcus J. Ranum) writes...

>	Perhaps perfectly reasonable, but you'll get taken to the
>cleaners if you do it.
> 
>	Database vendors are starting to add "benchmark specials"
>into their servers - operations that are part of TPC that execute
>much more cheaply than you'd expect them to if configured for a
>benchmark. So if you benchmark a machine that is legitimately HALF
>as fast as another, against a machine that's TWICE as fast, but
>running a DBMS WITHOUT the "benchmark specials" the faster machine
>may lose. If you buy a system based on that measurement you've just
>been robbed.
> 

There is no doubt you have to go against this intelligently and with some
amount of skepticism.  TPC-A numbers, or any benchmark numbers, are a
starting point not an ending point.  A single data point can't possibly
tell you if YOUR application will run a particular way on a particular
machine, it can only narrow down those you might select amongst.  It is
certainly true that EVERY database system has capabilities which are
exercised/usable in some cases and not in others.  So you can never have
100% accuracy on trying to map a particular benchmark to your
application, but your buying decisions probably shouldn't be quite that
fine grained anyway (a 10% difference is probably required to be truely
meaningful).

One problem with your premise is that you assume "benchmark specials" are
that beneficial.  They aren't.  You'll note that DEC Rdb V6.0 on a DEC
7000 outperforms Oracle 7 running on the 10% faster DEC 10000.  DEC Rdb
does it without anything like Oracle's Discrete Transactions, which many
consider the prototypical "benchmark special".   I believe Oracle
introduced Discrete Transactions as a shortcut to a more universal
improvement within their product (ie, give an explicit and crude way to
cut some overhead now then go figure out how to more elegantly and
broadly give users the benefit in a future release).  The improvement is
probably on the order of 20%, not the 2:1 that your example implies.  So,
your hypothetical scenario isn't necessarily true. 

Certainly the bottom line is that you shouldn't use a single data point
to make a decision and you should question data points that are far out
of whack.  If machine X is twice the performance of machine Y on
benchmark B1, but equal on benchmark B2, then you need to ask the
vendor(s) why!

When it comes to comparing database systems there is precious little
reliable data out there beyond TPC-A/B numbers right now.  That means we
currently compare the performance of relational database systems with a
benchmark that tests about 5% (at best) of the capabilities of those
database systems.  You can compare with some other benchmarks (Wisconsin
and AS3AP for example), but the results of non-TPC benchmarks are
reported inconsistently.  The emergence of TPC-C and soon TPC-D will go a
long way towards providing more meaningful data points for comparing
database engines.  It will also be many years before benchmark specials
tarnish their usefulness.
