>[ Article crossposted from comp.unix.ultrix ]
>[ Author was Allen Leibowitz (leibowa@aa.wl.com) ]
>[ Posted on 24 Aug 1993 22:03:59 GMT ]
>
> - Can someone explain conditions that would cause a large number of 
>   "response timeout errors?  For that matter, what does this *really*
>    mean?  I know what the manual says, but when does this occur?

It occurs whenever a packet is lost in a manner invisible to the source. Lack
of buffering on the receiving node and problems with the physical plant are the
main causes.
 
> - Would it be unusual to see more multiple collisions
>   than single collisions?

No.

> - What is a "normal" percentage of errors on an ethernet as seen by
>   NCP on a VMS system?

This is kind of like "When did you stop beating your wife/husband?". Errors
are, by definition, abnormal. The "normal" error rate is 0. That said, there
are a number of things (probably including Heisenberg) that can cause errors in
the healthiest of networks. But they should be odd, random events and the
count should be near 0. For example, disconnecting and reconnecting a running
node will cause errors, but only a couple.

In the past month my 8650 with a lousy DEUNA has had exactly 2 errors. On the
other hand, my 6330 (DEBNI) had over 600. The 6330 had a bad crimp on the RJ45.
:-(  I would probably call the 8650 "normal", but the 6330 surely was not.

