RE: several
	I don't have a lot of time to go into this, but some factoids may
help;

- NSP has several flow control methods.
	DECnet-VAX uses "No Flow Control" which then falls on to a 
Send/Don't Send Link service messages.  (also often called XON/XOFF ala async)
The sender assumes the receiver can receive any number of segments until
he hears otherwise.

	The other methods, Segment Count and Message Count are credit based.
DECnet-DOS typically uses Segment Count (except on NETBIOS sessions).
DECnet-RSX is the last system I know that used Message Count.
I forget what DECnet-ULTRIX does.

- Acks can be piggy-backed on most messages.

- An NSP receiver Must ack a data message unless a "Delay Ack Allowed"
	bit is set in the transmitted header.

- VMS sets delayed-ack-allowed bits based on the number of media frames
	that can fit in the transmit pipeline.  I forget the exact algorithm,
	but increasing your PIPELINE will increase the delay ack window.

- Some bugs in this stuff have been removed in the past few years.
	Depending on version your observations may vary.

	Dave.
	Mr. DECnet-DOS (V1-V3).



