As the question has been asked before, and as it seems to generate a
        reasonable amount of response, I have tried to assemble some of
        the available data on the evolution of the PDP-11 into a SAQ
        (Sometimes Asked Question :-) ).

Most of my direct knowledge is of the earlier 11's and gets real hazy
        around the 11/5x - 11/9x boxes. However, the following people
        have helped fill in some of the gaps:

ARD#SIVA.BRIS.AC.UK (tony)
ron@topaz.bds.com (Ron Natalie)
BQT@SOFIA.DOCS.UU.SE (Johnny)
terry@spcvxb.spc.edu> Terry Kennedy, 
zrepachol@cc.curtin.edu.au (Paul Repacholi) 
tls@panix.COM (Thor Lancelot Simon) 
JOHNH@PSYCH.SU.OZ.AU

I Still have only superficial data on the 11/5x and 11/7x(micro)
        machines. Any input welcome.
        - Mike.McCrohan@iol.ie
------------------------------------------------------------------
              Evolution of the PDP-11 family of computers.

        During the late 1960s DEC had built a successful business based
        mainly on the success of its 12-bit PDP-8 series of
        minicomputers.  However, the time had come for the introduction
        of a 16-bit machine to replace/supercede the '8. Edison DeCastro
        designed a 16bit machine, but after a disagreement left DEC,
        formed DG, and built the initial success of that company on his
        16bit NOVA.

        DEC eventually countered with its entry into this market segment
        - the PDP 11/20. This machine was to father a whole industry;
        lay the foundations on which the VAX would later be built; and
        virtually launch the OEM industry attracted to the
        connectability and extendability of the UNIBUS, and later,
        Q-Bus. 
        
        Since then, the PDP-11 had 16 to 22 processors, depending on how
        you count them, many with variants. The following attempts to
        briefly track the evolution and progression.

1970:   11/20, 11/15 born. Small Scale integration. 4 or 8k mem, 32k max
        (although almost never seen). Core.  TTY console Papertape
        reader/punch typical I/O. Clock State cpu control. [All
        subsequent PDP-11 machines were to be microcoded]

1972    11/45 first of the highspeed processors that begat 11/50, 11/55,
        11/70 (1975). 11/45 Microcoded, SSI/MSI, memory bus, core or
        bipolar memory. Max Memory 256kb addressable via optional 18 bit
        Memory management unit. Optional Floating Point Unit 11/55 had
        special bipolar high speed mem. Fast peripheral to memory slots
        Split I&D spaces. Introduced some extra instructions such as
        MUL, DIV, ASH, ASHC, SPL.

1973(?) 11/55 had special high speed (300ns) bipolar mem. Floating point
        as standard (?). Kernel, Supervisor and User CPU operating
        modes. Otherwise identical to 11/45

        The PDP-11/45/50/55 had two Unibuses, normally joined by a
        single spaced jumper. The second Unibus has no arbitration, and
        was part of dual-ported fastbus memory. If you had fastbus
        memory (which made a 45 a 50 or 55), a second processor could
        access the memory. The only problem was that with the Unibus A
        and B split, DMA devices on Unibus A could not see the fastbus
        memory.  I had a PDP11/50 and PDP11/20 set up in such a fashion,
        with the PDP11/20 doing high speed A/D sampling and signal
        averaging using shared memory with the PDP11/50.

1975    11/70. CPU very similar to 11/45 in raw performance. Machine
        introduced with had core initially and MOS later. These machines
        introduced Cache, 22bit Memory management of up to 2Mb, Floating
        point processors, etc. Massbus periperal controllers which had a
        direct path to memory first appeared on this machine. 

1972   11/40, 11/35. Price same as 11/20, higher perf. Small- and
        Medium-Scale Integration. Architecture of cpu like 11/45 without
        the raw power. Memory Management Unit, EIS/FIS Extended and
        Floating instruction sets as options.  Introduced with Core
        memory, and later eith MOS.  Microcoded. Max Memory 256kb. Extra
        instructions such as SOB XOR MARK SXT and RTT introduced. MUL,
        DIV etc implemented in the EIS option.

1974 (ish) 11/05, 11/10. Perf same as 11/20, lower price. Microcoded.
        2-board processor.

1975 (ish) LSI-11 PDP 11 implemented on 4 chips. (Western Digital?) First
        of the Q-bus machines (All others above were UNIBUS.)
        Performance approx equal 11/05. Minimal size. Aimped at OEM
        imbedded controllers etc. 

1976 (ish) LSI-11/2 "Real) PDP11 on a microprocessor. (11/21?) Follow on to
        LSI11. First DEC CPU Microprocessor. Used the T(iny)-11 chip.
        Perf = 11/05, price much less. Qbus

1976 (ish) 11/03 Same microprocessor. Perf = 11/05, price much less.
        Qbus. More expandable than LSI11-2. Aimed at low end of 11/05
        market.
1976 (ish) 11/04 Same microprocessor.Follow on to 11/05. Perf = 11/05,
. 
