berenson@nova.enet.dec.com (Hal Berenson) writes:
>
>mjr@tis.com (Marcus J. Ranum) writes...
>>	Bear in mind that if you're comparing TPC numbers, that you
>>should try to compare one DBMS vendors TPC numbers only against their
>>own performance on other platforms. I.e.: Don't buy your hardware
>>based on RDB's TPC results on platform A versus Oracle's TPC results
>>on platform B.
>
>I disagree.  If you are trying to compare best hardware/software
>combination option 1 versus best hardware/software combination option 2
>then its perfectly reasonable to compare results where BOTH the hardware
>and software vary.

	Perhaps perfectly reasonable, but you'll get taken to the
cleaners if you do it.

	Database vendors are starting to add "benchmark specials"
into their servers - operations that are part of TPC that execute
much more cheaply than you'd expect them to if configured for a
benchmark. So if you benchmark a machine that is legitimately HALF
as fast as another, against a machine that's TWICE as fast, but
running a DBMS WITHOUT the "benchmark specials" the faster machine
may lose. If you buy a system based on that measurement you've just
been robbed.

	Let me put it in a different form: if you are benchmarking
a PC against a CRAY and the benchmark is to compute the first 100
decimals of pi, and my PC's software recognizes the benchmark and
just returns the right value from a store in memory - is that
cheating when it blows the CRAY away??  Conceptually, this is the
kind of maneuvers we are talking about - there are details, and the
vendors who play these games will tell you they're significant - but
they're cheating by any definition of cheating that I understand.

	If you're using TPC or any other benchmark to make an
assumption about the performance of a piece of *hardware* be
VERY VERY careful - because you're measuring the combination
of hardware + software. In order to get a meaningful measurement
you want one of those two to be a constant, and the hardware isn't.

	Perhaps you trust vendors to look out for your interest.
That's your option, but it seems counter-intuitive to me.

mjr.


