We have lots of experience making bootable systems using a variety of drives
(Fujitsu, HP, IBM, etc) all in the 1-2 GByte range.  The secret seems to be to
make sure the partition table is right very early in the installation.  Earlier
drives and earlier versions of Ultrix sometimes made you come up in single user mode or mount the disc on a working machine and manually calculate and enter a partition table.  All drives/versions of Ultrix used in the last couple of years seem to have calculated a partition table based on characteristics written on the drive by its manufacturer and presented it for our approval (calling it RZxx) prior to building the system.  In all cases where the installation questions were answered correctly, the machi

nes booted without trouble.  Our
only problem in this area was on a flakey RZ57 which then died forever and
was replaced by a 1.6 GB Hewlett Packard drive.:-).
