In article <CBwuD1.F0G@wm.estec.esa.nl>, dave@bccsun1. (Dave Stafford x4437) writes:
> I was wondering how flow control is setup with DEC SCP. When 2 machines 
> setup a link, I see a flow control value (called MESSAGE CREDIT by the 
> NGC Sniffer) of one machine set at 11, and on the receiver of 0. Does
> this figure indicate the number of unacknowledged data segments that
> can be received by a host?
The 11 is the number of credits for that circuit, and the 0 is the number of
returned credits. ( From memory... )

> 
> I've also noticed that the receiving host acknowledges every packet
> sent. Is this normal, can't DECnet say have a window of 10, and ack
> the last packet received? How is the flow control decided?

The reciever will hold the akc till it has a message to pass back, it recs
a REP, or the circuit ack timer expires. This depends on the flow controll
type, but the segment flow control has been droped from use as I remember.

~Paul



