I've got yet more questions to throw into this discussion. What (in DECnet)
is meant by a Negative acknowledgement, and why would it be sent? 

I've done loads of testing on different systems (VAX 9000, Alpha, Suns etc.) 
and the changes several of you suggested for DELAY FACTOR and WEIGHT made
an incredible difference on the Suns across a lossy network; the time taken
to retransmit went from 4 seconds to about 150 mSecs. The difference in
performance was from 80 secs to transfer a 2.4 Mbytes file to about 5 secs.

I tested several combinations of the above NCP parameters, and Delay Factor=32,
Delay Weight=48 was the best compromise between performance and excessive retransmissions.

However, the changes made no difference whatsoever to the Alpha, it just sent
about 200 packets, and hung around for 10 secs, before doing it again.

I'm pretty sure that there is definitely a Cisco dropping packets in the net,
but that's bound to happen anyway, so are there any other parameters that
could affect performance in NCP. I noticed that the Alpha had only 10 receive
buffers for the ethernet interface, whereas the 9000 had 15. Also the Inactivity
timer on the VAXs is 60, whereas it is only 10 on the Suns, what does this
control?

Thanks for all the help so far, and Colin, I'll try and get you a full decode
soon.

