In article <1993Aug19.151644.23512@decuk.uvo.dec.com>, websterc@decuk.uvo.dec.com (Colin Webster) writes:
> In article <1993Aug18.094817.2529@cmkrnl.com>, jeh@cmkrnl.com writes:
> |>In article <CBwuD1.F0G@wm.estec.esa.nl>, dave@bccsun1. (Dave Stafford x4437) writes:
> |>> I've also noticed that the receiving host acknowledges every packet
> |>> sent. Is this normal, can't DECnet say have a window of 10, and ack
> |>> the last packet received? 
> |>
> |>The gotcha here is that there is no good way to determine what the "last
> |>packet" is.  Some protocols (LAP-B and X.25, both at levels 2 and 3) use a
> |>holdoff timer to avoid sending acks for a short time (so you have yet another
> |>pair of parameters that have to be gotten right).  However this design dates
> |>from the time when many data links were half-duplex with turnaround; the idea
> |>was to cut down on the number of line turnarounds.  In the absence of long
> |>turnaround delays the sending and receiving of an ack per packet isn't all that
> |>costly. 
> |>
> 
> 	I think I know what is going on here now, I believe Dave
> 	has the SUN/DNA implementation of DECnet, which is phaseIV,
> 	(NSP 4.0.0), whilst I have been looking at phaseIV+, (NSP 4.1.0
> 	or higher)
> 
> 	PhaseIV+ has a bit (12) in the SEGNUM field called the
> 	ack delay flag.  If this is set, then the receiver does not
> 	have to ack. This allows a transmit window of around 17 for
> 	default exec settings, however, the bit is cleared in the mid-point 
> 	packet to allow the target node to receive and process and send 
> 	an ack before the window is empty.
> 
> 	So the answer is, yes, DECnet can window if you have phaseIV+
> 	(from around VMS 4.7 ?)

This isn't the way I understand the term "window" to be usually used.   

NSP has always (well, at least since Phase III) been able to have a transmit
window > 1 (it can send packet n+x before getting an ack of packet n).  As I
understand it, In the current VMS implementation the transmit window size for a
new logical link is init'd to 2/3 of the pipeline quota (as expressed in
packets), and then adjusted according to flow control messages from the
receiver, timeouts, etc. 

I don't know if NSP supports a receive window size > 1.  A receive window size
> 1 means that the receiver can accept packets out of order.  This permits
selective retransmit, among other things, but restricts the transmit window 
size to truncate(n/2) of the number of available packet ids (otherwise there
are ambiguities in certain error conditions).  

Neither of these seems to me to be related to whether you can hold off sending
ACKs or not.  Is the relevant term "ack window" ?

