In article <CBuzFs.3rI@wm.estec.esa.nl>, dave@bccsun1. (Dave Stafford x4437) writes:

| I still have a question re. DECnet performance. Because packet delivery is not
| certain, DECnet must have retransmission timers built in. Why then does it
| seem to take upto 8 seconds for a sequence to be retransmitted? 
| 
| Can anything in NCP be changed to reduce the time taken to recover from lost
| packets?

There's a pretty good discussion of the subject in the _MultiNet System
Administrators' Guide_ by TGV.  It explains that NSP (DECnet IV's transport
layer) sets the retransmission timer to be the product of MAX (estimated
round trip time, 1 sec) and the NCP DELAY FACTOR.  

DELAY FACTOR, which is given in one-sixteenths, is by default 80, so the
default minimum retransmission time is 5 seconds.  TGV suggests that 
DELAY FACTORs in the range of 24 to 48 (i.e. 1.5xRTT to 3xRTT) 
are more reasonable than the default.  This would give you a minimum
retransmission interval of 1.5 to 3 seconds.

By the way, you might also look at the RETRANSMIT FACTOR, which is how many 
times NSP will attempt a retransmit before deciding that the link is dead.  With a
DELAY FACTOR value of 48 , this means that NSP won't give up for at least three
minutes.

Aaron

