In article <24prqh$91p@panix.com>, tls@panix.com (Thor Lancelot Simon) writes:
> Broken '44s are a great source of 8085s.  Actually, most of the broken DEC
> stuff I've played with lately could be salvaged by mixing and matching 8085s
> and 68000s between boards; 8085s for PDP and early VAX vintage Q and Unibus
> stuff, and 6800s and the occasional 68010 for later VAX Unibus boards.  Like
> the Ethernet board in my 750 that has a faster processor than the CPU...

  There's also 80186's (on the TQK50/TUK50, the RRD50 controller, etc.). And
don't forget the T-11 (DEUNA and other things) and the various things that
use 8051's (DEQNA, DHV11, etc.).

> The original poster left out the 11/93 and 11/94; 1990 vintage (introduced
> around the same time as the VAX 9000) and probably the last PDP, they're just
> the 83/84 CPU board with a higher clock speed.  There's a faster processor
> chip available but it may no longer be made; the Option/module used to list
> the DCJ11 at 25Mhz (I think), what's in the '93, and also a 30Mhz (again, from
> memory) DCJ11 with a _lower_ part rev level (like B instead of F or
> something); maybe this never worked right or something.

  Close. [I had the first 11/93 outside of DEC, BTW]. The 11/93 and /94 are a
new design which uses the J11 CPU. Instead of cache, the entire main memory
(2 or 4Mb) is on-board w/ 70ns parts. A new memory controller gate array lets
the CPU at the memory during NPR transfers (even during block mode). A Z80 CPU
controls 8 emulated DL ports (this subsystem is called a DLV22) with buffering,
etc. The board uses the same 18Mhz DCJ11-AE as the 11/8x - all the speed im-
provements are from a more modern board design.

  The 11/8x can't go much above 18Mhz. While the original design called for
20Mhz, neither the J11's nor the gate arrays were up to it. There were a lot
of neat features planned for both the J11 (higher speeds, WCS, CIS) and the
11/8x module (multiple boards with bus arbitration) that never worked out.
And of course, the MSV11-J modules had problems with read/modify/write. This
series of problems may be why DEC went outside for the 11/93 module and the
RQZX1.

  The 11/9x will go above 18Mhz. I've had mine up to 26.3Mhz. At that speed,
all the on-board stuff works fine, but Q-bus peripherals just go "huh?" as the
bits go whizzing past 8-). 22Mhz works fine, though.

  DEC claims to never have had parts faster than 20Mhz, while the chip vendor
has [informally] asserted that faster parts would be available - at a cost. I
understand this came quite close to litigation. Any faster part you see in the
Option/Module list would likely either be Category 1 (prototype) or 7 (cancel-
ed).

> The 11/84 and /94 are weird; if I remember right they're Q processors on a
> dedicated Q->Unibus converter.  Terry, do you know more about this?  I
> remember some dreadful hack of that general nature, but nothing more specific.

  It's not that bad a hack - the 83 and 93 boards were designed to support this.
The memory (for the 83) lives in the usual PMI slots, and then a bus adapter
brings the bus out to the Unibus. DEC realized that they had a lot of customers
with Unibus peripherals, but they didn't want to build another Unibus processor
(the previous one being the 11/24), so they expanded on the 11/24 + KT-24 con-
cept and came up with the 11/84. Considering that at that time, all DEC had on
the Q-bus was the RD-series and the KDA50 which started out as a real power
pig, Unibus was the way to go if you wanted a system with big (but genuine DEC)
disks and high-end tapes.

  Since then, everybody has entered (and exited!) the Q-bus controller busi-
ness. DEC has come out with the RQZX1 controller, which attaches the RZ-series
disks, TZ30 tape, and RX33 floppies to a Q-bus system, all on one board. This
is a PDP-11 only card (while it may work on a VAX, the resident setup code 
will only execute on a PDP-11). Given the shutdown of PDP-11 development and
support at DEC, this may very well have been the last PDP-11 CPU or option out
of DEC.

  Meanwhile, QED and Nissho are both showing non-J11 implementations of the
PDP-11 for various buses. Mentec is still (last I heard) building J11-based
boards (and adding Alphas). My 11/70 is still running strong (continuous power
on since 1975).

