dave@bccsun1. (Dave Stafford x4437) writes:
>Thanks to those who replied, my email has gone a bit strange in the meantime
>so apologies for not replying directly to you.
>
>I still have a question re. DECnet performance. Because packet delivery is not
>certain, DECnet must have retransmission timers built in. Why then does it
>seem to take upto 8 seconds for a sequence to be retransmitted? 

DECnet has a window adjustment algorithm to control congestion built into it.
It's not terribly different from the Nagel algorithm on the IP side, but unlike
Nagel in TCP, it can't be turned off. (For a demonstration, try running X11
over a circuit with Nagel turned on.)

This is compounded by the age of Phase IV and its Phase II and III antecedents.
It was designed in the days of slow, wide area links, to the parameters don't
readily lend themselves to LAN (or even fast WAN) environments.

>Can anything in NCP be changed to reduce the time taken to recover from lost
>packets?

Sure. But not enough to fix it because this all is controlled by exec
parameters which effect ALL circuits.

In order of significance:

1. Reduce the DELAY FACTOR. Either 16 or 32 are a big improvement.
2. Reduce PIPELINE QUOTA on fast nodes.
3. Increase the DELAY WEIGHT to 50 or 100.
4. Increase the RETRANSMIT FACTOR to allow for more retransmits.

The latter really only reduces the likelyhood of losing a circuit do to a
transient drop in the line and is probably not too significant. The first will
greatly enhance interactivity, but will reduce throughput on things like file
transfers.

Pipeline quota can be used as a throttle to cut the number of back-to-back
packets sent by a fast system. If you are seeing a large number of system
buffer unavailables in the line counters, this MAY help.

DELAY WEIGHT is a poorly documented cousin of DELAY FACTOR. I won't swear to exactly
how effective it is since I have been unable to figure out exactly how it
works.

