In article <23srpq$1qo@huon.itd.adelaide.edu.au> mferrare@physics.adelaide.edu.au (Mark Ferraretto) writes:
>We are looking at upgrading our old 4/280 to either a 2 CPU SPARCserver 1000
>or a DEC Alpha 3000/500.  At the moment Sun looks better because we are 
>a mainly sun-based system (25 SPARCs and 5 DS 5000/120s) although we will have
>to "up"grade to Solaris 2.2 to run the 2 CPUs (we run 4.1.3 at the moment).
>But I have heard that it is easier to migrate from 4.1.3 to OSF/1 than what
>it is to go to Solaris 2.2.  Is this the case?

I just recently installed an Alpha running OSF/1. It took me *much* less
time to get it up and running, configured, and integrated into the local
network (including NIS/NFS services, etc) than it did the first time I
istalled a Solaris machine. On the other hand, the installation procedure
on 2.2 is greatly improved over the one on 2.1, so hopefully this will
continue and 2.3 should be better.

The only part of the migration which might (key word there) be a little
easier is in the admin/command/file-naming-and-placement knowledge, which
carries over a bit better from 4.1.3 into OSF/1 than into Solaris 2.x. 
However, IMHO, that's minimal... any competent admin (and most users)
should be able to pick up the differences (basically, between BSD Unix and
AT&T) easily enough. The AT&T-flavor is also used by other vendors, so
it's good knowledge to have.

In both cases you will have to obtain new version of commercial software,
and recompile PD sources. The binary compatibility mode in Solaris 2.x
should give you a little leeway, and buy you time to do this (mileage
will vary). With OSF/1 you'd have to do it 8 o'clock day one. Plus
you would have to buy new hardware.

Since you've already made a serious hardware investment in Sun's, it will
probably just be easier to stick with them for your current-use systems.
When you are ready to buy *NEW* systems to replace them... then come back
and ask again! :-)
