|>
|>Sure. But not enough to fix it because this all is controlled by exec
|>parameters which effect ALL circuits.
|>
|>In order of significance:
|>
|>1. Reduce the DELAY FACTOR. Either 16 or 32 are a big improvement.

I think 16 would prove too low in most cases and would result in excessive
retransmissions, delay factor is expressed in units of 1/16th so 16
really means 1. For this reason, it should not be set to anything lower
than 32.

|>2. Reduce PIPELINE QUOTA on fast nodes.

Agreed. The transmit window is the pipeline quota/exec buffer size
so for a default exec buffer size of 576 and pipeline of 10000 would give
a tx window of 17 packets. 

|>3. Increase the DELAY WEIGHT to 50 or 100.

this won't have much impact on LAN - LAN connections, but making the value
too large will have an adverse effect on WAN links.

|>4. Increase the RETRANSMIT FACTOR to allow for more retransmits.

But don't make it too big!

|>
|>The latter really only reduces the likelyhood of losing a circuit do to a
|>transient drop in the line and is probably not too significant. The first will
|>greatly enhance interactivity, but will reduce throughput on things like file
|>transfers.
|>
|>Pipeline quota can be used as a throttle to cut the number of back-to-back
|>packets sent by a fast system. If you are seeing a large number of system
|>buffer unavailables in the line counters, this MAY help.
|>
|>DELAY WEIGHT is a poorly documented cousin of DELAY FACTOR. I won't swear to exactly
|>how effective it is since I have been unable to figure out exactly how it
|>works.
|> 

From the NSP specs:-
			NODEdelay= NSPweight * NODEdelay +deltaT
			----------------------------------------
			        NODEweight + 1

	where NSPweight=the weighting factor
	      NODEdelay=estimated round trip delay
	      deltaT   =the sample round trip delay

	NSPweight controls how quickly the estimated round trip delay
	can change, and won't have much impact for local lan traffic,
	the delay should always be low!


The reasons behind packet loss should be determined and fixed otherwise
tweaking parameters may end up masking a problem rather than fixing
the root cause.
Lookin at Dave's original posting, I would still suspect his problem
is related to the target node or something in between being over-run.
The line counters on the target system, and also the interface counters
on the cisco should confirm this.

A bit of experimentation with pipeline quota on the sending node 
may be in order!


