ken@syd.dit.CSIRO.AU (Ken Yap) writes:
> We've got an old MicroVax II (actually has the label AIStation on it).
> When it powers up it goes through the hardware self-test and stops with
> the >>> prompt. At this point one has to type B to boot.
> 
> I'd like it to continue by itself. Power failures at night, etc. Also
> I'd like to to boot from DUA1:  instead of the default DUA0:.
> 
> How, if at all, can modify the boot sequence? Please email.

Autoboot is controlled by the little toggle switch on the back.  Flick it
to the "dot outside circle" position.

The boot device is selected by searching all removable media, then all
fixed media, then the ethernet for a valid boot.  (It also looks for a
boot PROM in QBus space, but I can't remember where in the sequence this
happens.)  To stop it booting from DUA0, you need to remove the link to
the boot file that it uses.

This is easy; the machine looks first for [SYSEXE]SYSBOOT.EXE and then
[SYS0.SYSEXE]SYSBOOT.EXE.  If you want to render the disk unbootable, 
simply delete SYSBOOT.EXE.  If there's an OS on the disk that you want
to be able to boot from in emergencies, make sure there is no
[SYSEXE]SYSBOOT.EXE; if there is, use SET FILE/REMOVE to get rid of the
link (*not* DELETE -- SYSBOOT.EXE is multiply linked).  Then, rename
[000000]SYS0.DIR to SYS1; the boot will then fail to find SYSBOOT, but
you'll still be able to reach it with >>>B/10000000.  (The 1 in the top
nybble tells it which [SYSn...] to boot from).

--
