> In article <24rv8rINNm02@seurat.syd.dit.csiro.au>, ken@syd.dit.CSIRO.AU (Ken Yap) writes:
> =We've got an old MicroVax II (actually has the label AIStation on it).
> =When it powers up it goes through the hardware self-test and stops with
> =the >>> prompt. At this point one has to type B to boot.
> =
> =I'd like it to continue by itself.
> There's a toggle switch that handles that.  It's the red toggle switch on the
> back.

It's actually black, but it's next to a red LED display.  Maybe that's what you
meant.

> 
> =Power failures at night, etc. Also
> =I'd like to to boot from DUA1:  instead of the default DUA0:.
> 
> If I recall correctly, the uVAX-II will try to boot first from a tape, then
> from the disks, in sequence.  So if DUA0's not bootable, it should
> automatically move on to DUA1.

Almost, but not quite.  According to the KA630-AA users guide, page 3-13, the
boot device search sequence is as follows:

	a. A bootable removable disk (e.g. floppy)
	b. A bootable fixed disk
	c. TK50 tape
	d. Network (DEQNA, DELQA)

Devices are searched in order, so Carl is correct in that if DUA0 is not
bootable, the system should boot from DUA1. 

