In article <CBwuD1.F0G@wm.estec.esa.nl>, dave@bccsun1. (Dave Stafford x4437) writes:
> I've also noticed that the receiving host acknowledges every packet
> sent. Is this normal, can't DECnet say have a window of 10, and ack
> the last packet received? 

The gotcha here is that there is no good way to determine what the "last
packet" is.  Some protocols (LAP-B and X.25, both at levels 2 and 3) use a
holdoff timer to avoid sending acks for a short time (so you have yet another
pair of parameters that have to be gotten right).  However this design dates
from the time when many data links were half-duplex with turnaround; the idea
was to cut down on the number of line turnarounds.  In the absence of long
turnaround delays the sending and receiving of an ack per packet isn't all that
costly. 

