No, not really.  Indeed, Cutler would probably give you a withering stare
(or worse) if you used the word "architect" to describe him (he was well
known as loathing the concept of software architects.)

Dave Cutler may have been the "project leader" for version 1 of VAX/VMS, but
his role was more to make sure that the project got done on time rather than
to "architect" it.  In my opinion, there was no one "architect of VMS" - it
was a combined effort of many talented people, including Dick Hustvedt and
Tom Hastings, just to name two.  Cutler did have strong influence in some
of the design features of VAX and VMS, such as the 16-bit length limit for
character strings and DCL. 

I realize that it is a popular notion that Dave Cutler was "the father of
VMS", but my personal view is that he was more of the "obstetrician".  Dave
was (and evidently still is) tremendously talented at motivating engineers and
getting a product out the door, and he has had a large number of good ideas for
operating system design, but I believe that the "architecture" which
made VMS as successful as it was resulted from the efforts of other people.
