Folks,

  Martyn is quite correct. There are any number of third party SCSI drives that
Ultrix will load onto with no problem. All of the DS5000 series machines use an
NCR53C94 SCSI controller chip and the implementation is ANSI SCSI-2 compliant.
The standard Ultrix SCSI driver will see you through most of the storage device
types, but if you're looking to have Scanners/Printers/Other_Non_Storage_Device
items hooked up as well, you may want to look at using DEC's CAM SCSI interface
(an option on Ultrix v4.2b, but I think it may be the default interface now...of
course that's just from memory).

  There are several devices which may not behave properly,

	eg.  There was a vendor that had disk drives responding with a valid
	     "floppy disk" (Mode Page 5 if I remember) entry, which you don't
	     usually do on a hard disk, but with a size of 0. They had done
	     that for some other system vendor to be compliant with a base
	     system firmware. The valid mode didn't cause a problem, but when
	     Ultrix-boot saw a size 0 it would stop.

One thing to check is to inquire of the drive supplier if they have a DEC
specific variant for their firmware (many drive vendors do have DEC, SUN, HP
ROM variants) for the drive you are interested in. But I believe you'll be 
reasonably successful with most of the major brands.

I hope that helps.

