Summary: cc -O2 ignores pipeline issues?
CC: wchang@cshl.org

I am implementing a very tight innermost loop consisting of six integer
"MAX" operations, 20 instructions altogether.  While the C compiler produced
very tight code, using conditional-moves for "MAX", it apparently does
not optimize instruction scheduling.  For example, it compiles

  if (a>b) b=a;
  if (c>d) d=c;
to
  cmple a,b,$0
  cmoveq $0,a,b
  cmple c,d,$0
  cmoveq $0,c,d

which disrupts the pipeline (instruction latency plus register $0 bottleneck)
and causes 50% performance degradation.  I had to rewrite the C code as

  x=(a>b);
  y=(c>d);
  if (x) b=a;
  if (y) d=c;

to get the Alpha to perform near its spec.  Even then, the compiler still
improperly rearranged some instructions.  I had to undo this in the assembly
output of cc, after the loop was unrolled 4x4 = 16 times!  Also, cc -O2
does not seem to use all available registers.

If anyone knows how I can obtain a different compiler (GEM?), or an
instruction scheduler, I would appreciate very much the information.
The program instr-sched.c at gatekeeper.dec.com does not do I/O, otherwise
it might solve my problem.  I am using an AXP 4000/610 server running 
OSF/1 V1.2 (Rev. 10).

Thanks in advance,


