VS3520 and VS3540 are multiprocessor VAXstations (or servers) with two or
four CPU's. Models are quite old, and they were among the first CVAX 
processor based systems when they were published. I think that the year
they were announced may have been as early as 1988 or 1989.

Some of the vs35x0 models have graphics controllers which were state of the 
art those days. Those controllers are usually 24 planes (or more) and True
Color. Also these systems were first VAXstations having SCSI bus.

As the CPU is first CVAX model, the performance isn't too high compared
to new RISC systems, and if I recall it correctly, they are 2.7 VUPS
(VAX Unit of Performance) CPU's. 

And now your questions:
- Size of the machine is deskside, about same box as VAX 4000 desk side 
  model these days
- CPU power using SMP is about 2x or 4x that 2-3VUPS performance. SMP on
  these systems is well functioning and robust
- power is standard wall outlet
- VS35x0 refers to VAXstation, so there should be both keyboard and 19" 
  monitor. You can also use standard VT compatible terminal as directly
  attached console
- these systems are supported by OpenVMS and ULTRIX (VAX) operating systems

			-jari

