> We`re using VMS version 5.3.

  What model of 3100? If it's a VAXstation/VAXserver 3100 (definitely the
/30, /38, /40, /48 models and very probably the /76 as well), the boot
code doesn't know about 10-byte SCSI commands (needed to access disk space
beyond 1Gb). This can render a system unbootable if a file needed for the
initial system bootstrap is placed beyond 1Gb. This is the reason DEC lists
their > 1Gb disks as "data disk only" for these CPU models.

  However, in earlier versions of VMS, the VMS disk driver didn't know to
issue 10-byte SCSI commands and would instead use the 6-byte commands. This
would cause disk addresses to "wrap" at 1Gb. If VMS tried to write a file
at 1.1Gb into the disk, it would land at .1Gb in instead. I am not sure when
this was corrected, but I think that VMS 5.4-something had it fixed. I don't
know if V5.3 worked - the driver history uses sequential numbers, not VMS
version ID's. The CMS history for the driver says:

  *17   28-DEC-1989 09:51:11 KLUMPP "Merge 1GB corruption fix from hickory"
   14U2 22-DEC-1989 14:52:32 KLUMPP "Fix data corruptor for >1GB SCSI disks"

  (Jim Klumpp is the original driver author). So, if you were to ANA/IMAGE
SYS$LOADABLE_IMAGES:DKDRIVER.EXE and look for the string "image file ident-
ification" you could see if yours was X-14U3 or higher. If not, you're going
to need a VMS update.

