This was corrected with VMS V5.4-2.  Since then VMS has correctly dealt
with large SCSI disks.  I can verify this since I've been testing
large SCSI disks - up to 4 GB in size - under VMS as part of our
acceptable disk certification process for large disk buys.  In fact,
at the V5.4-2 point we wrote a program to explicit test for the "wrap-
around" problem which would affect some 1 GB disks but not other
depending on their detailed format (i.e. each vendor's 1 GB disk
is slightly different in size, total number of blocks, than another's).
We found that the Seagate Wren VII worked OK but a Fujitsu 1 GB
drive was a touch "too big".  We also verified that both drives
worked correctly under VMS V5.4-2.

A further note, be very careful of selecting a system disk for
a 3100 as the same 6- versus 10-byte SCSI command problem exists
in the ROM boot code.  A 3100 may not boot properly off a large
SCSI disk (>1 GB) depending upon where files end up on the
disk.  This could show up as "it worked yesterday but after doing
a standalone backup save/restore pass it won't boot anymore"
because the critical files needed to be read by the ROM code
are now out of reach on the disk.  Note that once enought of VMS
is loaded and switches the loadable driver the complete disk will
be normally accessible (assuming >=V5.4-2 that is).

