casper@fwi.uva.nl (Casper H.S. Dik) writes:

	Many ISVs shipped statically linked programs , they rather put their
	clients data at risk than having them see warnings that the C library
	they linked with was not suitable for the OS the clients were running.

In practice those warnings were often false alarms.  (Sun seems to have
turned most of them off in Solaris 2.2.)  Because of the false alarms,
in SunOS 4, it was often safer to ship statically linked binaries to
customers running the same or a newer release of SunOS.  I wouldn't
criticize the ISVs too heavily about this practice.

	Or they used some homegrown dynamic linking mechanism, rather than
	using the emchanism provided by Sun. (Such dynamic linking
	mechanisms often required static linking)

There are sometimes good reasons for this.  It may be more portable to roll
your own dynamic linking mechanism, and use it uniformly, rather than try
to use a common denominator of all the vendor-supplied dynamic linkers.

	The Solaris 2.[0-2] BCP mode shows two things:
		1) companies developing software for SunOS 4.x couldn't
		   follow standards/guidelines and just hacked away.

This criticism is a bit harsh.  Solaris 2.2 binary compatibility mode
doesn't always work even with programs that followed the rules, due to
some quiet changes in system call semantics between SunOS 4 and Solaris 2.
Since Sun didn't follow their own guidelines, why should we expect ISVs to?

	I heard some claiming that ``Solaris 2.x is a whole new
	operating system'' and charge accordingly.

I don't think such ISVs are necessarily out of line.  There are many,
many changes between SunOS 4 and Solaris 2, not just at the system call
level, but also in other important areas (e.g. access to printers).
Since the binary compatibility package in Solaris 2.2 is not reliable
enough for many production applications, significant work is sometimes
needed to do a high quality port.

Sun itself has delayed porting many of its own applications to Solaris 2.
