Come on people...  Enough is enough.  RMS does have a few limitations, but
it beats UNIX file handling hands down.  With RMS, you can create indexed 
files, relative files, and text files.  If you wanted this ability on a UNIX
system, you would have to purchase a software package or implement your own
file structures.  RMS has many other features as well.  DECnet file 
specifications can be used to access any file on any DECnet compatable computer.
On a UNIX system, the directory has to be either explicitly exported by a
NFS server or FTP the file to your local system.  For those of you that have
been moaning about the file specs not being uniform, you can use logicals
to simplify things.  In addition, RMS allows you to read and write to the same
file no matter if it is an indexed, relative, or text file.  It is unclear 
that you would ever have any hope of doing that on a UNIX box.  In addition,
VAXclusters allow you to share resources and files across several computer
systems.  VMS has so many features compared to UNIX it isn't funny.  UNIX 
systems are still playing catch up to become commercial quality systems instead
of being an AT&T hack.  UNIX seems to attract all of the sub-standard 
programmers.  If you can't program, you'll do UNIX!  For you UNIX guys, RMS
does have STREAM, STREAM_LF, and STREAM_CR files.  You can use VAX C, GCC, or
whatever and use those files just as you would on a UNIX box.  So, what's the
problem? 

