and will run more applications faster to completion though each will
individually take longuer to run.

   ALPHA:  the following is according to our Alpha support person:
	the OS is very BUGGY, new releases keep coming out very regularly and
	they do tend to improve things. Though one in particular they were never
	able to install and had to go back to the older release!
	   He had trouble porting software due to the 64bits pointers, but
	generally succeeded, though a lot of X11R5 code he never succeeded
	in making work correctly.  He keeps mentionning how similar
	OSF/1 is to Solaris 2.x!  He makes sure to use gnu tools rather
	than the systems one to get around the bugs in the system and
	for compatibility reason with public domain software.
	    As for third party software, DEC has started paying penalties
	to us because the software is not arriving according to the schedule in
	our contract. 
	   [[Personally, I would expect third party software to
	be ported to Solaris 2.x much faster than to OSF/1 because of the
	ratio of machines that run/will run Solaris 2.x vs OSF/1, even HP
	has abandonned OSF/1 and is moving very quietly towards SVR4.]]
	   Another point to mention, the binaries under the Alpha's are HUGE,
	there is a very good reason why DEC is selling 1GB disks with them.
	You'll need lots more SWAP than on a Solaris 2 machine for example
	for about the same mix of software that I use, he has 150MB of swap
	while I only need 50MB (you need to take into account that I have
	64MB of RAM).
	On the plus side, the machine are really fast and they do mesh
	fairly well with other Unix machine: NIS and NFS.  But then that's
	the least to be expected.
	      The alpha CPU tends to be faster than the Viking SPARC, but a 
	multi-processor Sun sells for less and will run more applications 
	faster to completion though each will individually take longuer to run.

   Solaris:  Once properly patched, the OS is very stable but don't expect
	to use Solaris 2.x directly from the CD-ROM: install those patches!
	   Most programs I ported myself in less than 2 days, for
	example the ftpd from wuarchive took me 1 afternoon.  The only
	applications I gave up on where the one that addressed the network
	directly (ex: xntpd), but then I have no knowledge of network
	programming.  Now those applications can be had directly over the net
	prepared for Solaris 2.
	    So as a user they are very few notable differences between
	Solaris 1.x and 2.x, slight touches to .cshrc and that's it.
	    The most difference occurs for the administrators and they
	usually all tend to be improvments.  But change scares a lot of
	people, so first they critize and then they quietly approve :-)
	    For example, when we received our first LX, most administrators
	here complained about having to install Solaris 2.x.  Well a couple
	of months later when Solaris 1.1C is available on the LX, nobody
	was interested in getting it!  Which leads me to think that Sun
	made two mistakes:
		1- releasing the Classic/LX without Solaris 1.x
		2- working on it and releasing it after we got over the learning
	           curve :-)

	    For the programmer, one needs to learn the POSIX and SVR4
	system interfaces, but since that leads to more portable programs,
	we believe that it is a good thing in the middle term.  For example,
	our effort in porting CAP to Solaris 2.2 immediatly paid off because
	now both SGI and HP offer the same low level network interface.  In
	the past CAP would not run native appletalk on those two types of 
	machines.

   So my recommendation would be to stick with Solaris machines unless you
really need the extra CPU power on a per process basis.

