I can't comment this of course, because this kind of commitments are
between you and the account resp. sales person.

>	   [[Personally, I would expect third party software to
>	be ported to Solaris 2.x much faster than to OSF/1 because of the
>	ratio of machines that run/will run Solaris 2.x vs OSF/1, even HP
>	has abandonned OSF/1 and is moving very quietly towards SVR4.]]

HP by themselves introduced OSF AES compatibility on HP-UX V9. Also
I've seen technology release of HP's OSF/1 on some customer sites. Of course
I can't give any commitments on if they're going to support full OSF/1 or not.

I would like to remind though that DEC OSF/1 AXP offers different habitants,
You can get functionality that matches OSF AES, SVID (Svr3, Svr4), BSD 
and XPG. Also there has been program announcement of FULL SVR4.x support 
on DEC OSF/1, but being an unannounced product ... well, it shouldn't
take too long. Anyway I'd recommend programming using POSIX or XPG instead
of writing BSD or SV specific code.

>	   Another point to mention, the binaries under the Alpha's are HUGE,
>	there is a very good reason why DEC is selling 1GB disks with them.

More I read this more I think all comments are related to BETA version of 
the operating system, when shared libraries weren't available! Smallest
configurations (standalone) we sell seem to contain ~450MB's of disk, but
these may vary between countries.

These days Alpha binaries (even X11 and OSF/Motif included) are quite 
reasonable sized because shared libraries are default.


>	You'll need lots more SWAP than on a Solaris 2 machine for example
>	for about the same mix of software that I use, he has 150MB of swap
>	while I only need 50MB (you need to take into account that I have
>	64MB of RAM).

There are different swap configurations you can use. Under V1.3 swap usage
drops (some cases even dramatically), and you can choose "the right swap 
algorithm" to support your usage. Default swap algorithm is "eager" and uses
more swap than so called "lazy" algorithm, but because "eager" method is
safer to applications, it has been set as a default method. "Lazy" algorithm
is similar to what for example AIX uses, and it consumes much less real swap
space in most cases.


