In article <1993Aug3.182513.13606@colorado.edu>, dwing@uh01.colorado.edu (Dan Wing) writes:
> In article <52813@sdcc12.ucsd.edu>, xm9@sdcc12.ucsd.edu (Tony Burzio) writes:
>>I remember clearly getting emergency security patches for VMS to
>>close holes that allowed users to log in to system without a 
>>password.  
> 
> Could you supply more information about this?

  I had one of these a few years ago (VMS V4.6 timeframe), where if there
was a modem hardwired to a VAX multiplexor port (like a DMF32) and the
user typed ^S and hung up the phone, the next user to dial in *at the same
baud rate* and type ^Q would inherit the first user's process.

  DEC denied the existence of this problem repeatedly, going so far as to
get an account on the Pageswapper BBS to claim that I was fabricating the
story (since I had posted a Pageswapper article about it). Later, when the
"terminal driver mandatory security update" was shipped to all customers,
the cover letter alluded to my problem, saying "only seen during laboratory
testing". Hah!

  In general, most VMS security bugs are far more subtle than that.

  I don't really worry about malicious non-privileged users being able to
gain unauthorized privs on my VMS systems, but I do have less faith in my
Unix systems.

>>This needs to be fixed.  However, I also remember all those DECwindows
>>users out there who ported, and then months later were told that DEC 
>>was dropping support...
> 
> DEC hasn't dropped support for DECwindows, DEC only adopted Motif as the
> windowing standard for VMS.  The software that I had, which was written for 
> DECwindows, still worked after I upgraded to Motif.

  Well, things like VPA stopped working. True, there was a bizarre work-around
for the problem. And if you reported a DECwindows (non-Motif) bug to the CSC
after DW-Motif came out, you'd almost invariably get "it works in DW-Motif"
or "yup, it's a bug, it'll be fixed in the next DW-Motif update" with no men-
tion of any non-Motif fixes.

