	D. Perkins) writes:

	>In article <23nbte$i1q@news.acns.nwu.edu>,
	>jweiss@casbah.acns.nwu.edu (Jerry Weiss) writes...

	>>I love the ease of clusters over NFS myself, but I don't like
	>>having some/all machines crash after a network failure.

	>You can get around this even more by putting a local system
	>disk in each LAN connected by the WAN.

This seems like sort of a silly solution if you ask me.  As a system
manager of a VMScluster with >30 satellites, I don't want to have to
maintain >30 different versions of the system.

	>Since every NFS node has it OS on a local disk it is no
	>wonder that it hasn't got this problem,

Wrong.  Our Ultrix set-up uses a single server and six satellites.
Each satellite has a single disk for its swap space, but both the
node-specific (i.e. "/") and shared (i.e. "/usr", "/usr/users", etc.)
components of the filesystem are mounted from the server.  This is
very much like VMS splitting each nodes' filesystem into SYS$SPECIFIC
and SYS$COMMON.

	>and since the VMS cluster systems all operate off of
	>(typically) one system disk it is no wonder that they do have
	>this problem - on the other hand they have considerably more
	>free disk space due to the removal of N-1 copies of the
	>operating system in a cluster of N nodes.

Ultrix works this way too.  In fact, the ways that Ultrix and VMS
split the filesystems into specific and common areas is quite similar.

It is interesting to note, though, that I can take down the single
Ultrix server and all the other six Ultrix satellites simply hang
until the server comes back up (whether it takes five minutes or one
hour).

If I were to take down both of our VMS servers, the cluster would
crash (with the standard ~15 second RECNXINTERVAL interval).  Raising
RECNXINTERVAL does work, but you can end up paying for it if you have
certain types of cluster transitions.  I once had to wait fifteen
minutes for the cluster to unhang because I had forgotten to set
RECNXINTERVAL back to 15 seconds after some ethernet bridge updates
were performed.  We >KNEW< the VMScluster would return, but we simply
couldn't do anything about it until those 15 minutes were up.

	>NFS and VMSclusters are not the same thing. They are not even
	>supposed to be the same thing. No wonder they don't work the
	>same. What a surprise.

Indeed.  VMSclusters have benefits and drawbacks, as do NFS-served
file systems.
						.oO Chris Oo.

