Its not quite the same.   Everything on the cluster hangs when till the
network comes back (or RECXINTERVAL passes).  With NFS, as long as you
don't access the remote disk you don't have problem.   That's about the
only thing I like about NFS.

In our time critical applications, we dont set RECXINTERVAL to absurd
values and we don't depend on remote remote disks.  If we lose the network
(and who hasn't) we have things wired so our critical system usually
and quickly regains quorum and continues.

I still would like to know what in cluster function or design mandates that
systems crash when they lose quorum?

