	If you've got real-time requirements that place your organization
at risk from a lawsuit, you're crazy if you're using any kind of
distributed computing system that has design flaws such that it could
lock up at a crucial time. Having to put a system disk on each box is
pocket change compared to what a major medical malpractice suit can
cost. ;)

	Of course, in a tightly coupled NFS setup, you're lucky if your
mouse cursor will even respond, when you can't reach your server. It's
my impression that VMS is a little better about how it handles network
partitioning, and so forth.

	I'm a (recovering) UNIX bigot, and I have to admit I really
dislike VMS because of its hideous interface and stupid insistence on
using braindamaged terminals and function keys (not to mention the
dinosauric, baroque, applications)  - BUT - if I had to run a computing
centre with high uptime, ANY degree of fault tolerance, and robust
transaction processing, I'd buy VMS and choke back the vomit. UNIX
is *NOT* where VMS is, and probably never will be - which just shows
how mind-numbingly stupid most UNIX vendors have been about selling
their product. And they're wondering why everyone seems desperately
waiting for NT to come along as an alternative to UNIX...

mjr.


