Now, this is why I can't keep out of this mild flamewar any longer.
Anyone will half a clue will have to admit that NFS is a joke when
we start this thread.
For one thing, you can delete NFS files, even when someone have them opened,
and that person will the be a big time loser. The file really can be
deleted. If you start a program, and someone deletes it, maybe to install
a new version while you are running it, you crash like a big stone.
NFS don't have a clue about files really. It just pass stuff along.
Stateless. There are cludges to fix this. Deleting a file openeed
means NFS just renames it, and deletes it later. However, you can still
delete it locally, which is terminal. You have lockd under SunOS, which
are supposed to handle file locks (I manage to crash SunOS 4.1.1
every time while experimenting with lockd. Fixed in 4.1.2 though).
Still don't work at our school though. You can lock the whole file,
but that's about it.
Record locking don't work. (Or what it's called now again, don't remember,
and actually don't want to know. It's bad enough in my mind to not want
to use it anymore).
