That's the difference between a WAN structured distributed network and a
tightly coupled VMScluster -- Folks here have been quick to point out that
these distributed methods of access have two entirely different goals.


OpenVMS supports NFS and Network access to it's file system(s;-) via DECnet
or other network protocols but the VMScluster provides the aversion to 
disks and servers going away for seconds at a time because of one thing 

               "RELIABLE DATA ACCESS".  


You can increase VMScluster parameters to resemble the lapses of 
communications typically found in WAN environments but that defeats the
purpose of having a VMScluster: 

-- To protect and provide ABSOLUTELY reliable access to the Data 
ANYWHERE IN THE VMScluster (which can then be served out to other 
workstations, PCs, or second tier servers...).



Where Most PCs are measured in hours to reboot, Stand alone boxes are 
measured in Days, weeks or even months (with a UPS and great Systems
Staff;-), Production OpenVMS Clusters measure continual uptime in years
with no interruption of service and no loss of data availablity to the 
user community. (*when properly configured and maintained*).

Can VMSclusters be DEtuned to resemble NFS or a network file access.

Certainly, but a better approach would be to use a TCP/IP product with
NFS if that's the level of reliablity that's required.

(And of course, layering on an NFS product onto a VMScluster gives 
 NFS users that high level of absolute reliable data access too 
 -- even if the some of the network links are severered for minutes at
 at time.)

Call ME a fool but today I just came back from a manufacturer that has 
had continous uptime of their VMScluster since 1991 and today we upgraded
and replaced two older 6000 class machines with two new 7000 class machines 
running OpenVMS. 

We did this at 10:00am without taking the cluster down or interrupting service
to their 300+ concurrent users who are on the system 24 hours a day, 365 days 
a year.  Well maybe I'm bragging a little;-) (we took an 8 second state 
transition twice when we booted the replacement machines and it did take us 
two hours to let the final batch jobs finish on the older machines before 
we could decommission them;-)

Colleges, Internet nodes, and Archive sites, often don't often require this 
level of reliablity and in these situations other distributed schemes may be 
more suited than a VMScluster.

You want to tie things togeather -- Use NFS, FTP or Kermit 
(and they works on OpenVMS too!)

You want to create a nonstop distributed working environment with the 
highest availablity in the industry -- Use a VMScluster...

