In article <23ocrj$r1q@news.acns.nwu.edu> jweiss@casbah.acns.nwu.edu (Jerry Weiss) writes:
> 
>    I still would like to know what in cluster function or design mandates that
>    systems crash when they lose quorum?

As others have noted, systems don't crash when they lose quorum.  They hang.
And they resume operation when quorum has been regained.

In the situations being described, however, quorum isn't the whole story.

If you get a network outage that lasts longer than RECNXINTERVAL, the
cluster splits.  The nodes on each side decide that the nodes on the other
side have gone away.  Accordingly, each half of the cluster kicks the
nodes from the other side out of its cluster database.  The distributed
lock databases on each side are rebuilt, dropping all locks held by
nodes that are no longer in the cluster.  (Actually, I'm not sure whether
the lock database rebuild takes place immediately or is deferred until
quorum is regained).

At this point, one half of the cluster is certain to have lost quorum and
the other side may have lost it as well.  In previous versions of VMS,
it was possible to cleverly configure quorum disks such that both sides
could regain quorum.  I am told that this is no longer possible.

Now if the network outage is fixed, both cluster halves discover each other.
VAX clustering does not have the capability to merge two clusters.  There
are a number of problems that would make any such capability quite difficult
to implement.  In the absence of such a solution, the resolution of
the problem is for machines in one (or both?) sub-clusters to crash with a
CLUEXIT bugcheck -- "node voluntarily exiting VAXcluster" until only one
cluster remains.  If a machine that is already in a cluster discovers the
existence of another cluster with the same cluster number/password then
the machine is crashed in this way with a CLUEXIT bugcheck.

In article <TWILLEY.93Aug4125202@dewey.nl.nuwc.navy.mil>,
 twilley@dewey.nl.nuwc.navy.mil (Jack Twilley) responds:

> I had this question as well, and spoke with a faceless yet helpful
> person from tech support.  They explained to me that each OS-serving
> machine (machine with a boot disk) gets a 'vote'.  This way, when there 
> aren't enough there, it waits until it gets enough 'votes'.
> Makes sense to me.

Well yes, that's close to what's going on.

The votes are allocated by the system manager.  He can put as many votes
on each machine as he likes.  The intent is to avoid the phenomenon known
as a "partitioned cluster".

In a partitioned cluster, you have a cluster in which half of the nodes
cannot talk to the other half.  Aside from the lack of communication and
the fact that you can't combine the two halves without getting a CLUEXIT
bugcheck, this isn't a big problem -- _unless_ you've got some sort of
cluster-wide shared resource.  In particular, shared, multi-ported disk
drives are a problem.  If the two halves of the cluster still have
a path to the same disk, then they would cache and update the disk data
without the coherency that a cluster-wide lock manager allows.

Because of the _very_ serious consequences of updating a shared disk
from a partitioned cluster, DEC adopted the quorum scheme.  A cluster
with half or less of the expected number of cluster votes (set in part
by the SYSGEN parameter EXPECTED_VOTES) is not allowed to do anything.
It hangs.  A cluster with more than half of the expected number of votes
can safely assume that no other collection of nodes can form a cluster
and access the shared disks (the other cluster would hang for lack of votes
if the system manager hasn't outsmarted himself by having a too-small
value for EXPECTED_VOTES)

Quorum disks are a feature where cluster members use a shared,
multi-ported disk drive as a low-speed network connection, polling it to
periodically re-validate the [non]membership of other cluster nodes.
The disk contributes votes toward quorum as long as it is cluster-
accessible and does not indicate the presence of non-member nodes. 

Here's hoping I'm neither boring the Unix weenies nor about to be toasted
by the VMS gurus more experienced than myself.

	John Briggs			vaxs09@v7.vitro.com


