In Article <23944v$60k@iskut.ucs.ubc.ca>
twong@civil.ubc.ca (Thomas Wong) writes:

>I have heard a number of stories from those who got rid of their VAXs in
>exchange with what is available nowadays, from Suns to SGIs to HP, to
>IBM...etc. My experience with VAXs were with the really old models of
>several genertions ago (when I was still an undergrad) so I have no idea
>what the new models are like nowadays. I've heard stories from... VAXs
>are lacking so far behind the others that it's just not worth it, to
>they don't make VAXs anymore, only decstations. 

Nonsense.  DEC still make VAXes, and also now AXP machines (which can run
VMS or OSF/1).  The two are different architectures (CISC vs RISC) so
performance comparison is not straightforward.  If your job is numeric
intensive, I think the AXP would probably be better value.  For general
timesharing, you'd better do a benchmark of your application.

DEC regularly releases new more powerful computer models, just like everone
else.  DEC is a competitive company --- it is definitely keeping up.   Note
that competetiveness is a variable thing, subject to medium-term and
short-term cycles (the leapfrog effect) --- it definitely pays to keep an
eye on the long-term trends.  VAXes first became popular due to
their good value, but DEC lost that lead in the late 80's.  They have come
back with a vengeance in the 90's, with the VAX 4000 series and the AXP
machine in particular being excellent value for money.  No-one can say what
the future will hold, but I reckon that DEC would be a safe bet to stay an
industry leader.

> I've also herad plenty
>of horror stories about aftersale service from DEC and how it costs more
>to service a part than to buy a new one. 

This can happen with any vendor, if the equipment is old enough.  Because
maintenance costs are usually calculated as percentage of purchase price,
eventually it becomes cheaper to replace the equipment with a new model
rather than continue to maintain the old equipment.  This *does not* mean
that you have to replace it with equipment form another vendor!

> ANd way above the prices of the other systems like Sun, SGI...etc....

What prices?  The maintenance prices, or the new equipment prices?  I doubt
you would find either of these to be true.

>The reason why I'm asking is, we are wanting to buy a specialized
>controller/analyzer for a piece of hardware in one of our labs. One of
>the companies that we are considering offers their software along with a
>VAXstation 4000. They have no spec on it but it comes with the whole
>package and the price is good. So I'd thoght I'll ask for
>experience/opinion from those on the net and perhaps have experience
>with a VAXstation 4000 (at least some specs).
>So first of all, what is a VAXstation 4000? Is it obsolete? Are VAXs
>still being built/supported? Does it cost way more to servive aftersale?
>How do you find VAXs in general (nowadays)? We have other companies we
>are considering but would like some feedback on this first. Are the
>other makes that much better (Sun, SGI, ...etc)? Thanks in advance.

I've got an older model (VAXstation 3100), as well as some VAX 4200
computers.  All are very reliable, and cheap to maintain.  Annual
maintenance is about 10% of purchase price, which includes rights to new
versions of VMS (media and doc distribution is extra).

If the price for the VAXstation 4000 package is good and it does what you
want, then go for it.  Don't pay attention to the ignorant scuttlebutt that
you have been fed.


