The DEREP was Ethernet V2. The DEMPR is virtually identical to the DESPR
(except for having 9 ports instead of 2). The diagram I have DOES show the
DEMPR cascaded off of the DELNI, but it also shows the DELNI connected to the
backbone with an H4000-B transceiver. This was a modified version of the H4000
which had heartbeat disabled for exactly this purpose.

I think one big problem with handling heartbeat is that for almost all cases,
Ethernet is totally plug and play with VERY little knowledge of gory details
required. So the odd cases (and the repeater is the oddest) cause massive
confusion.

Most types of networks REQUIRE special knowedge to do most everything and, as a
result, people are far less confused by minor incosistancies. In a token ring I
think everything is slightly inconsistant. :-)

I guess were all sort of spoiled by the simplicity of Ethernet.

