> set up.  I know, I tried.  NFS blows it out of the water.  Did you
> know that you need to put the password into the file path in VMS if you
> have the file protected on the other machine?  Some security.

You're comparing apples to an empty basket.  Unix simply doesn't do what VMS
(and TOPS-10 and TOPS-20 and RSX and RSTS and RT and whatever ran on PDP-8s)
can do:  give you access to files across the network *without setting up
anything* (except the network itself) in advance.  In effect you're doing the
export/mount on the fly and tearing it down again when you're through.  In a
network with thousands of nodes, you can't mount everybody's disks all the
time, so this kind of casual access makes sense in some circumstances.

If you want to do a fair comparison, try comparing NFS with DFS, or FAL with
FTP.  (I notice that FTP still comes with Unix systems, even though it requires
you to put the password in.  Some security.)

If you don't like sticking passwords in the filespec, you can set up a proxy,
which is equivalent to the hosts.equiv method that we all know is so secure.
:-)
