In article <2318ug$1hs@controversy.math.lsa.umich.edu>, khillig@U.Chem.LSA.UMich.EDU (Kurt Hillig) writes:
> 
> That said, "officially" 1 VUP = 1 MIPS = 1 SPECmark; the benchmarks are all
> scaled relative to an 11/780.  (MIPS is actually an absolute number, but
> the 780 was very close to 1 MIPS for a "standard" instruction stream.)

Actually, the 11/780 would execute, on average, 500,000 VAX instructions
per second.  This was generally agreed to be about the same as 1,000,000
IBM 360 instructions/second, hence 11/780 = 1 VUP = 1 MIPS.

The MicroVAX II was generally thought to be about 0.9 VUP.  It had
a 200 ns microinstruction time, and the average path length for
a VAX instruction was 11 microcycles.  All perfectly consistent, but
still with the odd factor that a VAX instruction was worth two IBM
instructions, presumeably because the heart of the VAX was pure.
-- 
