In article <TWILLEY.93Aug4125202@dewey.nl.nuwc.navy.mil> twilley@dewey.nl.nuwc.navy.mil (Jack Twilley) writes:
>In article <23ocrj$r1q@news.acns.nwu.edu> jweiss@casbah.acns.nwu.edu (Jerry Weiss) writes:
>
>   I still would like to know what in cluster function or design mandates that
>   systems crash when they lose quorum?
>
>I had this question as well, and spoke with a faceless yet helpful
>person from tech support.  They explained to me that each OS-serving
>machine (machine with a boot disk) gets a 'vote'.  This way, when there
>aren't enough there, it waits until it gets enough 'votes'.
>Makes sense to me.
>

To paraphrase, you set up the system in such a way that you can live with the 
minimal number of systems/disks up and still operate.  You can set the system 
up so that you can run with only one machine up, but you have to be VERY 
careful about partitioning.  partitioning is when you have 2 "separete 
clusters" that think that they have sole access to your disks.  They may 
manage the locks internally ok, but they (the 2 clusters) do not talk to each 
other.  You will get data corruption eventually.  That is why your system will 
hang or crash if there are problems.  It is better to have the os take a 
powder, rather than risk data integrity.  Our systems are set up so that we 
can lose several voting and all non-voting members and still survive.  If your 
cluster hangs or crashes after losing one memeber, it may not be set up 
correctly.

This is all in laymans terms, but the concepts are basically correct.  Please 
do not flame me do death if I have made a mistake, but feel free to add/ 
modify what I've said.
