In article <CBAH74.78u@sugar.NeoSoft.COM>, karl@NeoSoft.com (Karl Lehenbauer) writes...
>In article <3AUG199323435176@gergo.tamu.edu> carl@gergo.tamu.edu (Carl D. Perkins) writes:
>>Since every NFS node has it OS on a local disk...
> 
>Where'd you hear that?  There are totally diskless workstations that work
>with NFS.  Due to the low cost of disks these days, though, popping a
>$200 200 megabyte disk into a box isn't much of a consideration.
>-- 

That was in reference to the "it keeps on running whereas the VMScluster hangs"
posting. In order to do that the NSF nodes have to have the OS local or,
at best, never attempt to access the system disk when the connection is
down.  This is a common NFS setup in a WAN environment, and not exactly
uncommon in a LAN environment.  Having multiple system disks in a
VMScluster is quite possible (and not all that difficult) but is rather
uncommon except in the case where not all systems in the cluster use the
same version of the OS, for whatever reason, or a mixed VAX and AXP cluster.

The actual point was that you can get a similar effect in a VMScluster
with a configuration that is designed to do so such as each LAN part of the
cluster having its own system disk and sufficient quorum to run when the
WAN goes down. The difference is that this is more or less the default way
NFS works but when using a VMScluster it needs to be deliberately configured
to do so.

--- Carl


