Unfornutely, your experience with hardware failures mirrors mine.  We bought
3 5000/200 in the beginning of 1991.  Two system boards failed within a month,
so we got a warrenty repair.  One of the warranty boards failed last month, so
in a sense I'm 3 for 3.  

I've got 2 alternative recommendations for you:

	Upgrade your 5000/200 to 5000/240.  It replaces the system motherboard
and gives you a separate CPU (a la the 5000/1xx series); you keep the Turbo
stuff, power supply, and memory.  The one 200 we upgraded last Spring is still
running -- whatever that reliability statistic is worth.  This improves 
horsepower by about a factor of 2 for very close to the same price you'll pay
just to get a replacement 200 system board.  Maybe you DEC account rep will be
sufficiently embarassed to give you some special financial consideration. . .
asking is cheap, and certainly my rep tends to be sympathetic in such cases.
(In fact, her reponsiveness to problems such as these has helped keep me as
a DEC customer; I have currently have a 20 DECStation farm in a group with
20 NEXTs and 70 SUNs.)

	Get a service contract on the box (not the display or anything else).
Normally, of course, these contracts aren't a good deal for the technically
competent, but with the 5000/200 series, it's much cheaper than paying list
for the repair parts this often.

	Of course, both of the above apply only to /currently working/ boxes.

