There is support for an inter-processor interrupt, for the "arbiter" CPU to
halt other CPUs, etc.  But to my knowledge there is no support in the memory
system for interlocked instructions. (Perhaps someone with a copy of the MS630
technical manual can provide hard data on this point.) 

Whether the interlocked instructions are there or not, the way I heard it,
through unofficial-but-usually-reliable channels, is that the KA630 is slow
enough that the overhead of dealing with an extra CPU would eat up a
significant fraction of one CPU's compute power, so this is not a good
tradeoff.  The KA630 design does provide for up to *four* processors, but no
Q-bus backplanes were ever built with more than four Q/CD slots, so it's
unclear where you'd put the memory in that case.  (Three CPUs and one 16 MB
memory module would fit, though.) 

> The question is: can VMS run on a 
> two-processor uVAX II? It's almost obvious that this thing is officially
> unsupported; however, is there any underground way to make it work and
> use both CPU's as it would in bigger VAXen?

If the interlocked instruction support is actually in the hardware, at minimum
you'd have to provide your own MP support code -- I suspect that it was written
and tried at one point, but I don't believe that it's in the
SYSTEM_SYNCHRONIZATION.EXE that DEC supplies. 

If there's no support for interlocked instructions, forget it. 

