In article <23tf9q$93d@agate.berkeley.edu>, avk@hafnium.cchem.berkeley.edu
(Tony Konashenok) writes:

|>It is known from MicroNotes that uVAX II CPU has hardware support for
|>multiprocessor configurations. The question is: can VMS run on a 
|>two-processor uVAX II? It's almost obvious that this thing is officially
|>unsupported; however, is there any underground way to make it work and
|>use both CPU's as it would in bigger VAXen?

  All Q22-bus MicroVAX processors can (obviously) run as the Q22-bus bus
  arbiter.  Some can be configured as an auxillary processor: the KA630
  used in the MicroVAX II is one such processor.

  The arbiter typically communicates with the auxillaries via CSRs and
  Q22-bus shared memory.  Auxillary bootstrap and load can be accomplished
  through various (user-written) means -- including bootstrapping the
  auxillary via the PROM bootstrap footprint.

  One of the best auxillary CPUs is likely the KA640 -- it has a reasonable
  quantity of on-board memory and it has on-board DSSI storage and network
  connections. (All MicroVAX processors lacking a local I/O nexus/adapter
  always have to go `through' the bus arbiter to get to the I/O peripherals.)

  These Q22-bus systems are not suited for symmetric multiprocessing under
  OpenVMS, as the only memory visible between the processors is the Q22-bus
  memory.  These systems can be used -- with user-written software -- for
  somewhat less tightly coupled multiprocessing systems.

  There exists further information on this topic located in the MicroVAX or
  VAX CPU Technical User's Guide -- for each microVAX or VAX processor that
  can be configured as an auxillary.  (The PROM bootstrap footprint is in
  the CPU documentation.)

  But as was mentioned, this isn't supported by OpenVMS.

