In article <23ocrj$r1q@news.acns.nwu.edu>, jweiss@casbah.acns.nwu.edu (Jerry Weiss) writes:
...
> I still would like to know what in cluster function or design mandates that
> systems crash when they lose quorum?
> 

It's because of a nasty problem call partitioning. This means your
cluster is split into at least 3 bits. ( it could be 2, but this tends
to prevent the realy nasty problems. )

 ==============**=========================**=============================
    |  |  |        |  |    |    |   |   |    |   |    |    |   |    |
   a1 a2 a3       B1 b2   B3   b4  b5  b6   c1  c2   c3   c4  c5   c6


This is a 3 segment ethernet, with 2 bridges ( ** ) and boot nodes
B1 and B3. If the bridges get their forwarding tables in a twist,
the SCS packets may not get from A to B to C. Now look at the case
where A can see B, but not C, B can see A and C, and C can't see A.
This could result in say c4 mastering a lock on a disk on b5 because
it is the only one accessing it. If a3 was to noew access that file,
these accesses would not be corectaly locked. Result, screwed file, or
soon after, screwed disk.

Note, the cluster should not crash, it should suspend *ALL* nodes
until quorum is re-gained.

If your cluster is critical, put all the SCS traffic on a net of its own,
and use second interfaces for LAT, IP and DECnet. Try to not put
"smart" routers into the cluster trafic path, and stick quorum disks
out in the boondocks of the wire to stop the rot.

There is a problem with the PE driver crashing vaxes under some
sort of network error. ( 5.5 and 5.5-2 ) This is a real pain.

Putting a cluster up is a real no-brainer. Putting it up so it
works well under faults and heavy load is not so simple...

~Paul



