>at least there are binary translators from its predecessors VAX and MIPS. No
>such thing for Solaris 2.x. Go out and buy new copies for all your third-party
>software...

That is not necesaarily true. However ISVs have made such a mess
of their software in general that it won't run on Solaris 2.x.

The blame doesn't lie solely with them, but mainly. Things that
Sun should have done before they marketed Solaris 2.x were:

	- see what applications are important for your customers.
	  run them in BCP mode.
	  Such a project could have been started a long time ago
	  and Sun could have helped ISVs making their SunOS 4.x
	  products run reliably on Solaris in BCP mode.
	- provide source code compatibiliy with SunOS 4.1.x.
	  Instead, they provided ``BSD compatibility from hell''.
	  Source code compatibility within certain limits, of course.
	  But being able to simply compile a number of programs that
	  compiled w/o a hitch under SunOS 4.x, put the source
	  on Solaris, compile w/ /usr/sunos4/cc and run.


That being said, we can now return to our regular ISV bashing campaign.
I do not wish to offend the ``good guys'' among you.
And the statements below do not only apply to ISVs, but to a lot
of people who wrote software for in-house use with an attitude
of ``it works, don't care if it's portable''.
Remember it wasn't Sun who broke the software, it was whoever
wrote the software in the first place.

Many ISVs shipped statically linked programs , they rather put their
clients data at risk than having them see warnings that the C library
they linked with was not suitable for the OS the clients were running.
(Solaris 2.3 will run statically linked a.out executables)

Or they used some homegrown dynamic linking mechanism, rather than
using the emchanism provided by Sun. (Such dynamic linking
mechanisms often required static linking)

Or they didn't even to bother to read the lists of no-no's Sun
published (don't do this if you want to run on Solaris).

Or they programmed for SunView/Pixrect 6 (SIX!) years after Sun said
that they should switch to X based toolkits.

The Solaris 2.[0-2] BCP mode shows two things:

	1) companies developing software for SunOS 4.x couldn't
	   follow standards/guidelines and just hacked away.
	   [If you don't see what I mean, extrapolate from the
	    installation procedures that come with the various
	    products]
	2) SunSoft underestimated 1)

There are of course some exceptions. Since the object file format and
even the assembler file format has changed, compilers and debuggers
for Solaris 1.x will not work on Solaris 2.x.
The kernel has changed, so device drivers must be rewritten as well.

But I guess that a binary translater from MIPS to alpha won't
magically make the MIPS compilers output alpha object code.
Or compile MIPS kernel modules to Alpha kernel modules?

In our case the bulk of the third part software is exactly that:
compilers and device drivers. Binary translation won't help us.

Fortunately, a number of ISVs/resellers have provided us with
upgrades for Solaris. But I heard some claiming that
``Solaris 2.x is a whole new operating system'' and charge
accordingly.

If you can't write portable software, you're in the wrong business.

Casper


