I did a really interesting experience with NFS the other day. After
NFS-mounting a disk on a VAX/VMS host from an RS/6000, I accessed the
files on a Mac via the RS/6000

           Mac -> Ethershare -> RS/6000 -> NFS -> VAX/VMS

Now, on the Vax, I created a file A.A;1 with an editor. I went over to the
Mac and double clicked on the same file in order to edit it. Next thing I
saved a new version on the Vax, A.A;2. Now I tried to save the file from
the Mac (A.A;1, but got an error message stating that the file was read-
only and could not be saved). After quitting the Mac editor, I went over
to the Vax and PURGE'd A.A. Next I typed 'DIRECTORY A.A'. 

            Guess what I found...


No A.A whatsoever !!!

This is what I call sharing files over a network !

Since VMS PURGE'd version one of A.A, my only conclusion is that the NFS
server (or someone involved :-) erased version 2 when quitting from the
Mac editor. Nice behaviour, but what else is to expect from mixing three
different filesystem/os/nos...

BTW, we have TCPWARE as TCP/IP on the Vax, AIX 3.2.3 and S VII. 

